  "track": "track-02",
  "title": "ASP.NET Core memory distributed hybrid response and output caching",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "inventoryComplete": true,
  "concepts": [
    "Caching stores a previously computed value so a later request can avoid work. Every cache needs an explicit key, ownership boundary, expiration or invalidation rule, and a plan for stale, missing, or unavailable entries.",
    "IMemoryCache and IDistributedCache store application data; HybridCache combines an in-process primary cache with an optional distributed secondary cache and stampede protection. Cache-aside is an application flow, so a cache miss still needs an authoritative source.",
    "Response caching follows HTTP cache directives and client/proxy semantics. Output caching is server-configured response reuse and uses endpoint-aware policies. They have different eligibility and variation rules and must not cache user-specific or sensitive responses without a safe partition.",
    "Cache invalidation, key design, serialization compatibility, and concurrency behavior matter more than simply selecting Redis or memory. Expiration bounds staleness but does not make mutable data globally consistent; tags and versioned keys are logical invalidation tools whose behavior depends on the cache implementation."
  ],
